\paragraph{Epoch confound implications for the D\&R literature.}
Our controlled analysis reveals that ${>}$95\% of D\&R's reported PPL advantage is attributable to multi-epoch exposure, not filtering quality.
Under fully controlled conditions (matched epochs \emph{and} independent LR schedule), D\&R filtering actually \emph{degrades} performance relative to uniform training ($\Delta{=}{+}0.72$), indicating that the filtering step alone provides no benefit at our scale and contamination level.
This finding has broader implications: published detect-and-remove results that compare filtered subsets trained to convergence against unfiltered baselines at the same step count are confounded by the same mechanism---surviving data is seen multiple times, and the resulting multi-epoch exposure drives the improvement.
Researchers evaluating D\&R methods should report content epochs alongside step counts, and epoch-matched controls should be standard practice.

\paragraph{When uniform beats reweighting.}
